body:
@rsanchezsaez Will this Vision Pro support for Godot have eye-tracked foveated rendering in immersive experiences?

This wasn't possible before through Metal, but it seems an unreported change was made and Unity does now have some sort of eye-tracked foveated rendering support for fully immersive Metal Unity games. This can be seen in the new update to Demeo:
https://www.uploadvr.com/demeo-graphics-upgrade-foveated-rendering-on-apple-vision-pro/

@aaronfranke You raise very good points. I think it's worthwhile asking the community if we want to adopt the SwiftUI app lifecycle for iOS too. That would mean raising the minimum deployment target from iOS 12.0 to 14.0. I think it's an acceptable tradeoff, given 14.0 is over 4 years old now, and the SwiftUI lifecycle is the recommended way going forward. Even if we adopt it, we can keep the current `CAMetalLayer`-backed `GDTViewController` as currently implemented, and we could adopt Swift incrementally in separate PRs. I'll open a Proposal about this after this PR is merged to properly discuss the topic.

But I agree with @stuartcarnie that this PR should come first. This one is focused on code-reuse and creating the `Apple Embedded` and `visionOS` platforms. The advantage of doing this one first is that if the community decides we don't want SwiftUI lifecycle for the iOS platform, then this is still a worthwhile improvement.

@Grublady Great to see SCons is adding support for Swift independently. We have implemented a similar Swift builder, that would be added to the visionOS (and optionally shared with iOS) `SCSub`.

@huisedenanhai  Thanks so much for your detailed reviews. You caught a lot of enhancements and issues on the build changes.

I have resolved the conflicts from https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/105621 by further reusing the generate bundle methods by moving them to `platform_methods.py` and specializing them on the iOS and visionOS platforms.

As part of this I also:
- Removed the `ios_triple`/`visionos_triple` build option. It was only used to find `libtool` when cross-compiling from `macOS`, and I think it's no longer applicable with Xcode running on Apple Silicon macs (there's a single `libtool` binary).
- Ranamed the `ios_simulator` and `vision_simulator` build options to just `simulator` for consistency. I kept `ios_simulator` as a deprecated option for compatibility with existing build systems (it prints a build warning asking to move to the `simulator`option).

@huisedenanhai @Repiteo Could you take a look at the new commits? Thank you!

@kisg Thanks for the info! Unfortunately I won't be at GodotCon this year, but I hope we can meet some other time in the future. I'll keep an eye out for your PR.

@huisedenanhai Thanks again for the in-depth reviews, I think I addressed all the comments.

@MattParkerDev and anybody else interested in this: @BastiaanOlij made great points. I think SwiftUI is not a direct concern to this specific PR. I'm happy to discuss in depth in a followup proposal and/or PR.

@BastiaanOlij Let me know how the testing goes. Thanks for your time! Also looking forward to any feedback from the Core iOS team regarding this PR.
